<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https:\/\/schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Communities Unlimited test my well water?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. As part of a no-charge assessment, a Communities Unlimited technician collects a water sample and sends it to a certified third-party laboratory. You get the results and a clear explanation of what they mean."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does well water testing and a septic assessment cost?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Nothing. Communities Unlimited offers private well water testing and septic assessments at no charge to rural homeowners across the seven states we serve."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is my well water safe to drink?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If you are on a private well, no one tests it for you, so the only way to know is to have it tested. Well water can carry bacteria, nitrates, or arsenic without any change in how it looks or tastes. A no-charge Communities Unlimited assessment tests your water and identifies any contamination."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What can contaminate a private well?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Common sources include bacteria such as E. coli, nitrates from fertilizer or septic systems, naturally occurring arsenic, and problems with the well itself like a cracked casing or a poor seal."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which areas does Communities Unlimited serve?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Communities Unlimited works alongside rural homeowners across Arkansas, Oklahoma, Tennessee, Louisiana, Mississippi, Texas, and Alabama."}}]}</script>
